TITLE:: RLRelationContext
summary:: A list of objects and the named relations that move them: Cube Dance, its analogues, any family of chords
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLRelation, Classes/RLCategory, Classes/RLGraph, Classes/RLChordContext, Classes/RLVoiceLeading, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
Where an link::Classes/RLChordContext:: moves every chord to one chord, a relation may offer several, or none. A relation context answers the same questions (code::names::, code::transform::, code::act::, code::neighbours::), code::act:: with a list of any length; it also gives the monoid its relations generate (link::#-category::) and their graph (link::#-graph::).

link::#*cubeDance:: is Douthett and Steinbach's graph with its edges named, after Popoff: the 24 major and minor triads and the 4 augmented triads of 12 steps, with

table::
## P || n major - n minor || keeps an augmented triad
## L || n major - (n + 4) minor || keeps an augmented triad
## U || n major - the augmented triad on n; n minor - the one on n - 1 || six triads for each augmented triad
::

Each moves one voice by one semitone. In a division of 3k steps the same three relations join the two nearly even triads and the even one: 35 chords in 15 steps, 42 in 18, 56 in 24.

code::
~cd = RLRelationContext.cubeDance;
~sp = ~cd.space;
~aug = ~sp.chord(0, \aug);
~cd.act(\U, ~aug);                     // C major, E major, Ab major, C# minor, F minor, A minor
~cd.act(\P, ~sp.major(0));             // [C minor]
~cd.neighbours(~sp.major(0));          // [[U, C aug], [P, C minor], [L, E minor]]
~cd.category.size;                     // 40

// a path and its word (Muse, "Take a Bow")
~path = [~sp.major(2), ~sp.chord(2, \aug), ~sp.minor(7), ~sp.major(7), ~sp.chord(3, \aug), ~sp.minor(0), ~sp.major(0)];
~cd.wordOf(~path);                     // [U, U, P, U, U, P]

// its graph: distances, shortest walks, walks through every chord
~g = ~cd.graph;
~g.distance(~sp.major(0), ~sp.major(6));        // 6
~g.shortestPath(~sp.major(0), ~sp.major(1));    // C major, C aug, C# minor, C# major
~g.countHamiltonianCycles;                      // 1296

RLRelationContext.cubeDance(RLEDO(15)).objects.size;      // 35
RLRelationContext.triads(RLEDO(19)).category.size;        // 38: P, L, R as relations, a group
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: objects
argument:: relations
An Array of code::[name, relation]:: pairs; in place of an link::Classes/RLRelation::, a rule code::(x, y) -> value::.
argument:: quantale
Boolean when nil.
argument:: space
The link::Classes/RLChordSpace:: of the objects, when they are chords.

METHOD:: cubeDance
Cube Dance in an link::Classes/RLEDO:: of 3k steps (12 when none is given), with U, P, L. Any other system is an Error.

METHOD:: triads
The 2n triads nearest 4:5:6 of an equal division under P, L, R, each a relation with one image: the chicken-wire graph in 12 steps.

METHOD:: singleStep
The chords of a space, each once, joined when the voices of one reach the other by given moves (code::[1]::, one voice by one step, when not said); the relation is named S. Douthett and Steinbach's graphs for any family of chords.
argument:: space
argument:: moves
argument:: name

METHOD:: powerTowers
Power Towers: the 36 dominant, minor and half-diminished seventh chords of 12 steps and the 3 diminished sevenths, one voice by one semitone. 39 chords, 72 edges; the steps between two chords are their least voice leading, for all 741 pairs.

METHOD:: pipeline
Pipeline (Douthett and Steinbach): the 24 dominant and half-diminished sevenths of 12 steps, two voices by one semitone each. 108 edges, nine at each chord: Childs's nine operations, six wechsels and three schritts of the chord context of the two types (link::Classes/RLChordContext#-wechsel::).

METHOD:: preserving
Noll's monoid of a chord: the maps x -> ux + v of an equal division's pitch classes that send a set of them into itself, each named as code::'3x+7'::. Eight maps for C major in 12 steps, generated by 3x + 7 and 8x + 4.
code::
~noll = RLRelationContext.preserving(RLEDO(12), [0, 4, 7]);
~noll.belonging([0, 4, 7]);     // 1 for C, E, G; 0.875 for Eb; 0.625 for Ab, B, C#, F#, A, Bb; 0.375 for D, F
// as control data: louder the nearer a note is to the chord
Pbind(\note, Pseq((0..11)), \amp, Pseq(~noll.belonging([0, 4, 7]) * 0.2), \dur, 0.25).play;
::

METHOD:: pitchClasses
The pitch classes of an equal division under transposition and inversion, named code::\T0::, code::\T1::... and code::\I0::, code::\I1::... (I5 sends x to 5 - x), with T1 and I0 as generators. Klumpenhouwer networks live here (link::Classes/RLNetwork::).

METHOD:: networks
Networks as objects: the networks a network becomes under some moves, and the moves as the relations between them. A network on this context is a network of networks, valid as any network is: each move takes the network at its tail to the one at its head, and two routes compose alike. The result is a context like another, so a network of networks of networks is one step more (link::Classes/RLAutomorphism#*onNetworks:: carries the moves up).
argument:: seed
An link::Classes/RLNetwork:: of a relation context.
argument:: moves
code::[[name, move], ...]::. A move is an link::Classes/RLAutomorphism:: of the seed's context (contents and labels move together: one image), or anything else link::Classes/RLNetwork#-transformedBy:: takes (an link::Classes/RLTimeMap:: for a metre network); or an automorphism of its category alone, an Array as link::Classes/RLCategory#-automorphisms:: lists them and link::#-isography:: names one (Lewin's <T1>, <I5>: the labels move, and every valid network with the new labels is an image, twelve of them for T and I).
argument:: max
More networks than this is an Error: the moves must bring the seed back after some steps (a shift of time never does).
argument:: generators
The names that generate the category (all the moves when nil).
code::
~pcs = RLRelationContext.pitchClasses;
// a cell, C#, Eb, G under I4, I10, T6, and a plan with the cell's own labels: the notes are moved by I2, I5
// and T3, which move the labels as <I4>, <I10> and <T6> do
~cell = RLNetwork(~pcs, [[\x, 1], [\y, 3], [\z, 7]], [[\x, \y, \I4], [\y, \z, \I10], [\x, \z, \T6]]);
~moves = [[\i2, RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 11, 2)], [\i5, RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 11, 5)], [\t3, RLAutomorphism.affine(~pcs, 1, 3)]];
~hyper = RLRelationContext.networks(~cell, ~moves);              // 8 networks, a group of 8 moves
~plan = RLNetwork(~hyper, [\x, \y, \z].collect { |n| [n, ~cell] }, [[\x, \y, \i2], [\y, \z, \i5], [\x, \z, \t3]]);
~top = ~plan.fillings(\x, ~cell).first;                          // the network of networks that starts on the cell
~top.objects.collect(_.objects);                                 // [[1, 3, 7], [1, 11, 7], [4, 6, 10]]
~top.isValid;                                                    // true: I5 after I2 is T3
::

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: isography
The automorphism of the category that sends the generators, in their order, to these arrows (names, words or relations), as one of code::category.automorphisms::; nil when there is none. What Lewin writes <T1> or <I5> for T and I, where T1 and I0 generate:
code::
~pcs = RLRelationContext.pitchClasses;
~pcs.isography([\T1, \I1]);      // <T1>: every T kept, I(n) sent to I(n + 1)
~pcs.isography([\T11, \I5]);     // <I5>: T(n) sent to T(-n), I(n) to I(5 - n)
~pcs.isography([\I0, \T1]);      // nil
::
They compose as T and I do: <I9> after <I8> is <T1>.

METHOD:: objects
METHOD:: quantale
METHOD:: space

METHOD:: system
The space's pitch system.

METHOD:: names
Every name the context knows.

METHOD:: generators
The names given at the start.

METHOD:: transform
A relation by its name.

METHOD:: identityTransform
Each object related to itself.

METHOD:: arrow
An arrow from what names it: a relation, a name, or a word (an Array of names in the order of time).

METHOD:: nameOf
The name of an arrow: the first name of the context that does the same, else its word in the generators (read right to left), else nil.

METHOD:: define
Names the relation a word does (steps in time order).

METHOD:: act
What a relation offers from an object: a list.

METHOD:: neighbours
The objects one generator away, as code::[name, object]:: pairs (the object itself left out).

METHOD:: labels
The generators that relate x to y.

METHOD:: wordOf
For a list of objects, the first generator relating each to the next (nil where none does).

METHOD:: isWalk
Whether a word fits a list of objects, step by step.

METHOD:: truthValues
METHOD:: belonging
How far each object belongs to a set the relations keep: for each object, in their order, the arrows of the category that bring it into the set, and their share of all the arrows (1 for the members of the set). A graded membership, to use as control data.

METHOD:: union
The join of some relations (all the generators when none is named).

METHOD:: category
The link::Classes/RLCategory:: the generators generate, kept once made.

METHOD:: graph
The link::Classes/RLGraph:: of some relations.
argument:: which
Names; all the generators when nil.
argument:: cost
As for link::Classes/RLGraph#*new::.
